INTERPRETATION (GLM, 2026-09-21)
- Draft run: legacy migration shape proven (ADD COLUMN leaves r10/r15 NULL);
  final continuation query there hand-typed "<= 2" instead of the captured
  cutoff (=1) — superseded by the clean runs below, which use \gset cutoffs.
- ROUND A (Astra's leak schedule, REPEATABLE READ late writer): stale
  snapshot max=0; other writer committed ordinal 3; fence captured cutoff 3;
  late INSERT allocated the GLOBAL nextval 4 (snapshot-independent — the
  Round-2 MAX design would have reused the stale max and leaked); the
  continuation at the captured cutoff excludes lateRR_Q | 4. LEAK CLOSED.
- ROUND B (allocate-first, SERIALIZABLE): allocator took ordinal 8 and held
  it uncommitted; the fence waited (entry 12:04:19.53Z, acquired
  12:04:23.50Z, ~3.97 s), captured cutoff 8, and the continuation INCLUDES
  lateS_R | 8 — a member committed before the boundary. Both fence orders
  behave per contract.
- GUARD 1 supplied ordinal rejected; GUARD 2 legacy NULL->999 rejected
  (Astra's attack); GUARD 3 overwrite and NULL-out rejected, same-value
  write passes; GUARD 4 ordinary column update passes, rollback leaves a
  permanent unused value (7 after 5, never reused — harmless), legacy rows
  still NULL at final state.
- Probe-only holds used pg_sleep inside held transactions; the required
  qualification at Checkpoint B uses bounded pg_locks/pg_stat_activity
  observation instead of sleeps.
- Container rmt1040-r3-pg removed after the run. No AeroLink persistent
  database, repository state, or evidence outside this directory touched.
